export default '<div class="se-guide wrap">\n\n<nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">\n  <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Blog</span><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/self-employed">Self Employed</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><strong>The Ultimate Guide to Taxes For Self Employed</strong>\n</nav>\n\n<header class="mast">\n  <div class="eyebrow">PriorityPay &middot; Self Employed</div>\n  <h1>The Ultimate Guide to Taxes For Self Employed</h1>\n  <p class="standfirst">Learn what forms you need to fill out, where to send them, how to calculate how much money to set aside, what can count as a business deduction, deadlines, and more.</p>\n  <div class="stamp">\n    <span>Tax year <b>2026</b></span>\n    <span>Updated <b>August 2026</b></span>\n    <span>Figures from <b>IRS sources</b></span>\n    <span><b>Education, not tax advice</b></span>\n  </div>\n</header>\n\n<div class="filter">\n  <div class="filter-head">\n    <div class="filter-t">Four questions to sort this page</div>\n    <div class="filter-s">This guide is very long. Answering these questions will shorten this guide to show you only the sections relevant to your situation.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="q">\n    <label class="q-l" for="q-setup">Which of these describes your business?</label>\n    <div class="sel">\n      <select id="q-setup" data-q="setup">\n        <option value="">Choose one</option>\n        <option value="sole">I haven&rsquo;t registered anything &mdash; I just work for myself</option>\n        <option value="llc">I have an LLC and I&rsquo;m the only owner</option>\n        <option value="partners">I have an LLC with partners, or a partnership</option>\n        <option value="scorp">I&rsquo;ve elected S-corp treatment</option>\n        <option value="unsure">I&rsquo;m not sure</option>\n      </select>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="q">\n    <label class="q-l" for="q-year">Have you filed a tax return that included this income before?</label>\n    <div class="sel">\n      <select id="q-year" data-q="year">\n        <option value="">Choose one</option>\n        <option value="first">Not yet &mdash; this is my first year earning it</option>\n        <option value="unfiled">Not yet &mdash; I&rsquo;ve earned it before, but I haven&rsquo;t filed</option>\n        <option value="once">Yes, once</option>\n        <option value="more">Yes, more than once</option>\n      </select>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="q">\n    <label class="q-l" for="q-w2">Do you also have a job that pays you on a W-2?</label>\n    <div class="sel">\n      <select id="q-w2" data-q="w2">\n        <option value="">Choose one</option>\n        <option value="w2yes">Yes &mdash; self-employment is income on the side</option>\n        <option value="w2no">No &mdash; self-employment is all of my income</option>\n        <option value="spouse">No, but my spouse has one</option>\n      </select>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <fieldset class="checks">\n    <legend class="q-l">Check all that apply</legend>\n    <div class="checkgrid">\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="home"><span>I work from home</span></label>\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="drive"><span>I drive for work</span></label>\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="kids"><span>I have kids</span></label>\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="ownins"><span>I buy my own health insurance</span></label>\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="pays"><span>I pay other people to help with the work</span></label>\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="behind"><span>I&rsquo;m behind on taxes</span></label>\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="states"><span>I have clients in other states</span></label>\n      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" data-v="none"><span>None of these</span></label>\n    </div>\n  </fieldset>\n\n  <div class="reroute" id="reroute" hidden>\n    <b>This guide may not be the right one for you.</b> Once a business is taxed as an S-corp or a partnership, it files its own return and the forms below no longer describe your situation. The business owner guide covers that setup.\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="filter-foot">\n    <span id="filter-status">Showing all 39 topics.</span>\n    <span class="foot-actions">\n      <button class="reset" id="showall" hidden>Show the rest anyway</button>\n      <button class="reset" id="reset">Clear answers</button>\n    </span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class="toc" id="toc">\n<div class="toc-head"><span class="toc-t">What&rsquo;s in this guide</span><span class="toc-c" id="toc-count">9 sections &middot; 39 topics</span></div>\n<div class="toc-grid">\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="how-it-works">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#how-it-works"><span class="toc-n">01</span>How Taxes Work When You&rsquo;re Self Employed</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="nothing-withheld"><a href="#nothing-withheld">You&rsquo;re Responsible To Set Aside Tax Money Throughout The Year</a></li>\n<li data-ref="five-dates"><a href="#five-dates">You Have 5 Critical Tax Dates</a></li>\n<li data-ref="se-tax"><a href="#se-tax">You Pay Two Taxes: Income Tax And Self-Employment Tax</a></li>\n<li data-ref="same-1040"><a href="#same-1040">You File One Tax Return, Not Two</a></li>\n<li data-ref="llc-taxes"><a href="#llc-taxes">An LLC Does Not Change Your Taxes On Its Own</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="forms">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#forms"><span class="toc-n">02</span>Which Forms You Need To File</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="form-list"><a href="#form-list">Every Form A Self-Employed Tax Return Can Include</a></li>\n<li data-ref="schedule-c"><a href="#schedule-c">What Goes On Schedule C (The Business Income Form)</a></li>\n<li data-ref="issuing-1099s"><a href="#issuing-1099s">Forms You Have To Send Out When You Pay Contractors</a></li>\n<li data-ref="ein"><a href="#ein">When You Need A Business Tax ID Number</a></li>\n<li data-ref="what-to-gather"><a href="#what-to-gather">What To Gather Before You Start A Return</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="deadlines">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#deadlines"><span class="toc-n">03</span>Deadlines And How To Pay</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="all-deadlines"><a href="#all-deadlines">Every Deadline In A Self-Employed Tax Year</a></li>\n<li data-ref="safe-harbor"><a href="#safe-harbor">The Safe Harbor Rule: Avoiding A Penalty Without Predicting Your Year</a></li>\n<li data-ref="how-to-pay"><a href="#how-to-pay">How To Send Your Tax Payment To The IRS</a></li>\n<li data-ref="extensions"><a href="#extensions">An Extension To File Is NOT An Extension To Pay</a></li>\n<li data-ref="missed-payments"><a href="#missed-payments">What Happens If You Miss A Payment Or Spend The Tax Money</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="income">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#income"><span class="toc-n">04</span>What Counts As Taxable Income</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="no-form-still-income"><a href="#no-form-still-income">Income Counts Whether Or Not A Form Arrives</a></li>\n<li data-ref="nec-vs-k"><a href="#nec-vs-k">How Two Different 1099 Forms Can Report The Same Payment</a></li>\n<li data-ref="cash-basis"><a href="#cash-basis">Income Counts When It Arrives, Not When You Invoice</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="calculation">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#calculation"><span class="toc-n">05</span>How Your Tax Bill Is Calculated</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="standard-deduction"><a href="#standard-deduction">What The Standard Deduction Is</a></li>\n<li data-ref="brackets"><a href="#brackets">How Tax Brackets Work</a></li>\n<li data-ref="qbi"><a href="#qbi">The 20% Business Deduction (Qualified Business Income)</a></li>\n<li data-ref="two-taxes"><a href="#two-taxes">Putting It Together: The Two Taxes You Owe</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="deductions">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#deductions"><span class="toc-n">06</span>What Counts As A Business Deduction</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="no-official-list"><a href="#no-official-list">There Is No Official List Of Deductible Things</a></li>\n<li data-ref="categories"><a href="#categories">The Categories Most Business Expenses Fall Into</a></li>\n<li data-ref="home-office"><a href="#home-office">The Home Office Deduction: Who Qualifies, And Two Ways To Figure It</a></li>\n<li data-ref="mileage"><a href="#mileage">The 2026 Mileage Rate Changed Halfway Through The Year</a></li>\n<li data-ref="meals-travel"><a href="#meals-travel">Business Meals Are Generally 50% Deductible and Travel Is Generally 100%</a></li>\n<li data-ref="startup-costs"><a href="#startup-costs">Startup Costs From Before The Business Opened</a></li>\n<li data-ref="health-premiums"><a href="#health-premiums">Health Insurance Premiums Reduce Income Tax But Not Self-Employment Tax</a></li>\n<li data-ref="hobby-loss"><a href="#hobby-loss">When The IRS May Treat Your Business As A Hobby</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="credits">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#credits"><span class="toc-n">07</span>Which Tax Credits You Can Claim</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="credit-vs-deduction"><a href="#credit-vs-deduction">A Credit Comes Off The Tax. A Deduction Comes Off The Income</a></li>\n<li data-ref="child-credits"><a href="#child-credits">The Child Tax Credit And The Child And Dependent Care Credit</a></li>\n<li data-ref="eitc-savers"><a href="#eitc-savers">The Earned Income Credit And The Saver&rsquo;s Credit</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="set-aside">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#set-aside"><span class="toc-n">08</span>How Much Of Your Business Income To Set Aside</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="what-decides"><a href="#what-decides">What Decides The Percentage To Set Aside</a></li>\n<li data-ref="per-payment"><a href="#per-payment">Why Setting Aside Per Payment Is Better Than Saving Up Per Quarter</a></li>\n<li data-ref="also-w2"><a href="#also-w2">Taxes When You Have Both A Regular Job And Self-Employment Income</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="toc-sec" data-sec="after-filing">\n<a class="toc-s-h" href="#after-filing"><span class="toc-n">09</span>What To Do After You File</a>\n<ul>\n<li data-ref="records"><a href="#records">How Long To Keep Your Records</a></li>\n<li data-ref="irs-letters"><a href="#irs-letters">Most IRS Letters Are Not Audits</a></li>\n<li data-ref="amending"><a href="#amending">How To Amend A Return You&rsquo;ve Already Filed</a></li>\n</ul></div>\n</div></div>\n\n<nav class="mini" aria-label="Sections"><ol>\n<li data-sec="how-it-works"><a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a></li>\n<li data-sec="forms"><a href="#forms">Forms you file</a></li>\n<li data-sec="deadlines"><a href="#deadlines">Deadlines &amp; paying</a></li>\n<li data-sec="income"><a href="#income">Taxable income</a></li>\n<li data-sec="calculation"><a href="#calculation">The calculation</a></li>\n<li data-sec="deductions"><a href="#deductions">Business deductions</a></li>\n<li data-sec="credits"><a href="#credits">Tax credits</a></li>\n<li data-sec="set-aside"><a href="#set-aside">How much to set aside</a></li>\n<li data-sec="after-filing"><a href="#after-filing">After you file</a></li>\n</ol></nav>\n\n<section id="how-it-works">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">01</div><h2>How Taxes Work When You&rsquo;re Self Employed</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">How self-employment differs from a job with a paycheck, which taxes you owe, and what your business structure does and does not change.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="nothing-withheld" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">You&rsquo;re Responsible To Set Aside Tax Money Throughout The Year</div>\n<div class="row-s">For example, when a client pays you $5,000, you receive $5,000. However, a percentage of that payment will be owed in taxes. YOU are responsible to set aside that percentage for taxes.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>In a traditional job, the employer will withhold the money that goes to taxes, so that you never see or touch it. But as a self employed individual, it&rsquo;s your responsibility to set aside tax money.</p>\n<p>We&rsquo;ll discuss what you should do if you already spent your tax money for the year later in this guide.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="five-dates" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">You Have 5 Critical Tax Dates</div>\n<div class="row-s">Unlike an employee who only has one tax deadline (April 15), self employed people have four different payment dates plus the same April 15th filing deadline.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><h4>Why an employee only has one date</h4>\n<p>An employee&rsquo;s tax is paid every single payday, automatically, by their employer. There is no deadline for them to meet, because they never touch the tax money. By the time the year ends, most of what they owe has already been paid in small pieces, and filing a return is mostly a matter of checking the total and settling any difference.</p>\n<h4>Why you have five dates</h4>\n<p>You don&rsquo;t have an employer paying taxes on your behalf throughout the year, so the government asks you to send it four times a year instead. Those payments are called estimated taxes. Then you still file a return like everyone else.</p>\n<div class="figs forms">\n<div><span>April 15, 2026</span><span>Payment 1 for 2026, and the deadline to file your 2025 return</span></div>\n<div><span>June 15, 2026</span><span>Payment 2 for 2026</span></div>\n<div><span>September 15, 2026</span><span>Payment 3 for 2026</span></div>\n<div><span>January 15, 2027</span><span>Payment 4 for 2026</span></div>\n<div><span>April 15, 2027</span><span>Deadline to file your 2026 return</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>Notice that April 15 appears twice. It is the filing deadline for the year that just ended and the first payment for the year already running. These are two separate payments for two different tax years, leaving on the same day.</p>\n<p>If you pay contractors, there is a sixth date (January 31) by which you must send their 1099 forms.</p>\n<p>Every one of these dates, and what each one requires, is set out in <a href="#deadlines">Deadlines and how to pay</a>.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="se-tax" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">You Pay Two Taxes: Income Tax And Self-Employment Tax</div>\n<div class="row-s">Everyone who works pays both of these taxes. The difference is that a job splits the Social Security and Medicare bill with your employer, and self-employment does not. So you pay 15.3% where an employee pays 7.65%.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><h4>What self-employment tax actually is</h4>\n<p>Self-employment tax covers Social Security and Medicare. Therefore, it is not a penalty for working for yourself or an extra tax that employees escape. Employees&rsquo; Social Security and Medicare money simply comes directly from their paycheck.</p>\n<h4>Income tax is a separate tax</h4>\n<p>It works the same way whether you have a job or work for yourself. You pay income tax on top of self-employment tax.</p>\n<h4>How this compares to a job</h4>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>Social Security and Medicare &mdash; taken from an employee&rsquo;s paycheck</span><span>7.65%</span></div>\n<div><span>Social Security and Medicare &mdash; paid by that employee&rsquo;s employer</span><span>7.65%</span></div>\n<div><span>Social Security and Medicare &mdash; paid by a self-employed person</span><span>15.3%</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>At a job, the payslip shows 7.65% coming out. The employer pays a matching 7.65% that never appears on the payslip at all, so most employees never learn it exists. Working for yourself, there is no employer to pay that second half, so both halves are yours.</p>\n<p>That is the entire difference. An employee and a self-employed person both pay income tax, and both pay Social Security and Medicare. Only the split changes.</p>\n<h4>What makes up the 15.3%</h4>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>Social Security portion</span><span>12.4%</span></div>\n<div><span>&hellip; charged on profit up to</span><span>$184,500</span></div>\n<div><span>Medicare portion, with no upper limit</span><span>2.9%</span></div>\n<div><span>Extra Medicare charge above $200k single / $250k joint</span><span>0.9%</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>The Social Security portion stops once profit passes $184,500. The Medicare portion keeps applying to every dollar above that.</p>\n<h4>Two things that make the bill smaller</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Self-employment tax is charged on 92.35% of your profit, not the full amount. That number is 100% minus 7.65%, and 7.65% is exactly half of 15.3%. It exists so you are taxed on roughly the same base an employee would be: an employee&rsquo;s wages never include the employer&rsquo;s half of Social Security and Medicare, so self-employed people get to subtract an equivalent amount before the tax is applied.</li>\n<li>Roughly half of the self-employment tax you pay then becomes a deduction against your income tax. It lowers your income tax only &mdash; it never lowers the self-employment tax itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>You owe self-employment tax once your profit reaches $400</h4>\n<p>Income tax and self-employment tax are calculated from different starting points, and this is where that matters.</p>\n<p>Income tax is worked out after the standard deduction has been subtracted from your income. Self-employment tax is not &mdash; the standard deduction does not reduce it by a single dollar.</p>\n<p>So self-employment tax starts once your profit reaches $400, while income tax does not start until your income passes the standard deduction, which is $16,100 for a single person in 2026. Anyone whose profit lands between those two figures owes self-employment tax and no income tax.</p>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>Profit for the year</span><span>$16,000</span></div>\n<div><span>Self-employment tax owed</span><span>about $2,261</span></div>\n<div><span>Income left after the $16,100 standard deduction</span><span>$0</span></div>\n<div><span>Income tax owed</span><span>$0</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class="fine">Illustration only: a single filer with no other income, ignoring state tax and credits. If the numbers entered were true, this is what the calculation would look like.</p>\n<p>People in this position often assume they owe nothing, because they earned less than the standard deduction. That is true of income tax and false of self-employment tax, and it is one of the most common surprises for anyone earning self-employment income part time.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="same-1040" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">You File One Tax Return, Not Two</div>\n<div class="row-s">A tax return is the whole packet you send the IRS. Forms and schedules are the pages inside that packet. Working for yourself does not create a second return as it would for a business owner. You&rsquo;re submitting the same 1040 an employee would, but with a few extra pages (discussed below).</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><h4>Two words that should NOT be used interchangeably</h4>\n<p>A <b>return</b> is the complete filing you send in for the year. Most people send exactly one.</p>\n<p>A <b>form</b> or <b>schedule</b> is a single page, or set of pages, inside that return. One return can contain a dozen of them.</p>\n<p>So &ldquo;you only file one return&rdquo; and &ldquo;you fill out several forms&rdquo; are both true at the same time. They are not in conflict.</p>\n<h4>What your packet looks like</h4>\n<p>An employee sends a return containing Form 1040 and not much else. A self-employed person sends a return containing Form 1040 <em>plus</em> Schedule C for the business, Schedule SE for self-employment tax, and Schedule 1 for certain deductions. Same single return, more pages in it.</p>\n<p>Running two genuinely separate businesses adds a second Schedule C, which is also included in the same one return.</p>\n<h4>The exception</h4>\n<p>Two setups do create a genuine second return. A partnership files its own return, and a business that has elected S-corp treatment files its own return and pays you a salary. In both cases the business reports its own numbers separately, then sends you a statement (Schedule K-1) to carry onto your personal return.</p>\n<p>Here are a few examples:</p>\n<h4>A partnership</h4>\n<p>Two people run a design studio, $120,000 profit split evenly.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The studio files <b>Form 1065</b> (its own return) reporting the full $120,000</li>\n<li>The studio sends each partner a <b>Schedule K-1</b>. That&rsquo;s the statement, showing their $60,000 share</li>\n<li>Each partner reports their own $60,000 on their own personal return</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Three returns total: One for the studio, one for each partner.</p>\n<h4>An S-corp</h4>\n<p>One person, consulting business, $120,000 profit.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The business files <b>Form 1120-S</b> (its own return)</li>\n<li>It pays the owner a $70,000 salary and issues a <b>W-2</b> for it, exactly as any employer would</li>\n<li>The remaining $50,000 is reported on a <b>Schedule K-1</b></li>\n<li>The owner puts both onto their personal return</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Two returns: One for the business, one for the owner.</p>\n<p class="fine">Illustration only. If the numbers entered were true, this is what the filings would look like.</p>\n<p>Every form that can appear in a self-employed return is listed in <a href="#forms">Which forms you need to file</a>.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="llc-taxes" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">An LLC Does Not Change Your Taxes On Its Own</div>\n<div class="row-s">An LLC is not a corporation, and the IRS has no tax category called &ldquo;LLC&rdquo; at all. If you are the only owner, the IRS ignores the LLC completely and you file the same Schedule C as someone who never registered anything.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>This trips up almost everyone, because an LLC feels like it should change something. Legally it does. For taxes, by default, it does not.</p>\n<h4>Legal structure and tax treatment are two separate things</h4>\n<p>An LLC is a structure created by your <b>state</b>. A corporation is a different structure, also created by your state. They are not the same thing, and registering an LLC does NOT make you a corporation.</p>\n<p>The IRS is separate from the state. It does not have an &ldquo;LLC&rdquo; box on any forms. For federal taxes it sorts every business into one of three buckets: sole proprietor, partnership, or corporation.</p>\n<h4>Where an LLC lands in those buckets</h4>\n<div class="figs forms">\n<div><span>LLC with one owner</span><span>Ignored by the IRS. You file Schedule C, exactly like someone with no LLC.</span></div>\n<div><span>LLC with two or more owners</span><span>The IRS treats it as a partnership. The business then files its own return, separate from the owners&rsquo;.</span></div>\n<div><span>LLC that filed an election</span><span>Treated as an S-corp or C-corp, whichever was elected.</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>The official term for the first case is a <b>disregarded entity</b>, which is unusually plain language for the tax code: the IRS disregards the LLC and looks straight through it at you.</p>\n<p>The third case only happens if you actively file paperwork asking for it. It is never automatic, and nothing about forming an LLC starts that process.</p>\n<h4>Why do people believe LLCs can help save on taxes?</h4>\n<p>LLCs do not inherently help you save on taxes.</p>\n<p>The reason there&rsquo;s a misconception that an LLC can save money on taxes is that an LLC makes you eligible for an S-corp election.</p>\n<p>An S-corp can help certain people save money on taxes, and you can only make that election if you have formed an entity (an LLC or a corporation). Someone working for themselves with nothing registered can&rsquo;t make one.</p>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Go Deeper:</b> Learn more about the differences between <a href="/self-employed/sole-proprietor-vs-llc-vs-s-corp">sole proprietor vs LLC vs S-corp structures</a></div></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="forms">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">02</div><h2>Which Forms You Need To File</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">Learn which forms you need, what information each one asks for, and the forms you may have to send out rather than receive.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="form-list" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Every Form A Self-Employed Tax Return Can Include</div>\n<div class="row-s">Most self-employed people file Form 1040, the personal tax return, plus three attachments. More forms get added depending on which deductions and credits you claim.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>Most self-employed people file Form 1040, the personal tax return, plus three attachments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Schedule C</b> for business income and expenses</li>\n<li><b>Schedule SE</b> for self-employment tax</li>\n<li><b>Schedule 1</b> for deductions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More forms get added depending on which deductions and credits you claim.</p>\n<h4>Always, or nearly always</h4>\n<div class="figs forms">\n<div><span>Form 1040</span><span>The personal return everyone files</span></div>\n<div><span>Schedule C</span><span>Business income and expenses; produces net profit</span></div>\n<div><span>Schedule SE</span><span>Calculates self-employment tax from net profit</span></div>\n<div><span>Schedule 1</span><span>Adjustments &mdash; half of SE tax, health premiums, retirement</span></div>\n</div>\n<h4>Attached when relevant</h4>\n<div class="figs forms">\n<div><span>Form 8829</span><span>Home office, regular method only</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 4562</span><span>Depreciation and equipment expensing elections</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 7206</span><span>Self-employed health insurance deduction</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 8995 / 8995-A</span><span>Qualified business income deduction</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 2441</span><span>Child and Dependent Care Credit</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 8880</span><span>Saver&rsquo;s Credit</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 1040-ES</span><span>Estimated tax worksheets and payment vouchers</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 2210</span><span>Underpayment penalty, including the annualized method</span></div>\n<div><span>Form 4868</span><span>Automatic extension of time to file</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>Tax software usually attaches these forms based on the answers you provide. However, understanding which forms exist helps you ensure nothing is missed.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="schedule-c" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">What Goes On Schedule C (The Business Income Form)</div>\n<div class="row-s">Schedule C is where you list what your business does, everything it was paid before expenses, your expenses sorted into about twenty set categories, and your work driving. The profit left at the end gets copied onto Form 1040 and onto Schedule SE.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><h4>Information the form asks for includes:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Business name and address, if different from your own</li>\n<li>A principal business code. It&rsquo;s six digits describing the activity, chosen from a list in the instructions</li>\n<li>Your EIN if you have one. Otherwise, your Social Security number</li>\n<li>Accounting method (cash or accrual)</li>\n<li>Whether you materially participated in the business</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Income and expenses</h4>\n<p>Gross receipts go at the top, minus returns and any cost of goods sold, to give your gross income. Expenses are entered against named lines, like advertising, car and truck, contract labor, insurance, legal and professional, office expense, rent, repairs, supplies, taxes and licenses, travel, meals, utilities, wages, and an &ldquo;other expenses&rdquo; line.</p>\n<p>There are also two parts that collect vehicle information where car expenses are claimed, and home office details where the simplified method is used.</p>\n<p>The result is net profit or loss, which carries to Schedule 1 and to Schedule SE.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="issuing-1099s" data-t="pays">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Forms You Have To Send Out When You Pay Contractors <span class="tag">2026 change</span></div>\n<div class="row-s">Paying a contractor $2,000 or more during 2026 generally means sending them a 1099-NEC and filing a copy with the IRS by January 31.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>So you may receive this form from your clients, and also have to send this form to the people you contract.</p>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>1099-NEC threshold for payments you make, 2026</span><span>$2,000</span></div>\n<div><span>Deadline to send and file</span><span>January 31, 2027</span></div>\n</div>\n<h4>What you need to do it</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A Form W-9 from each contractor</b>, collected before paying them. It gives you their legal name, address and taxpayer identification number.</li>\n<li><b>A record of total paid</b> during the calendar year.</li>\n<li><b>A method to file.</b> The IRS accepts electronic filing, and most bookkeeping and payroll platforms will generate and file them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Important note</h4>\n<p>Only send a 1099-NEC for money you paid directly, like cash, check, or bank transfer.</p>\n<p>If you paid a contractor by credit card, or through an app like PayPal or Stripe, do not send them a 1099-NEC for that money. The card company or the app reports those payments to the IRS themselves, on a Form 1099-K. Sending your own form on top of that would report the same payment twice, and make it look like the contractor earned double what you actually paid them.</p>\n<p>Another important tip is to collect the W-9 up front BEFORE you contract someone. Chasing a contractor for a tax identification number in January, after the work has ended, is considerably harder than asking before the first payment.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="ein" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">When You Need A Business Tax ID Number</div>\n<div class="row-s">If you work for yourself with no employees, you can usually just use your Social Security number. An Employer Identification Number, which is a free business ID from the IRS, takes minutes to get and keeps your Social Security number off the paperwork you hand to clients.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>An Employer Identification Number is a business tax ID, required in some situations and optional in others.</p>\n<h4>When a Business Tax ID Number May Be Required</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>You have employees</li>\n<li>You operate as a partnership or corporation, or have elected to be taxed as one</li>\n<li>You have certain retirement plans, such as a solo 401(k)</li>\n<li>You file certain excise or employment tax returns</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>When a Business Tax ID Number is Optional</h4>\n<p>Sole proprietors frequently get a tax ID number anyway, because a W-9 given to a client asks for a taxpayer identification number. Giving a client an EIN is much less risky than a Social Security number. Banks also often ask for a business tax ID number when opening a business account.</p>\n<p>Applications are made directly with the IRS at no cost, and the number is issued immediately when applying online. Third-party services charging a fee are selling convenience, not access.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="what-to-gather" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">What To Gather Before You Start A Return</div>\n<div class="row-s">Before starting your tax return, collect your own income and expense records, any tax forms that were sent to you, a list of the tax payments you already made this year, and last year&rsquo;s return.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>Before starting your tax return, collect:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your own income and expense records</li>\n<li>Any tax forms that were sent to you by clients, payment apps, an employer, or the health insurance marketplace</li>\n<li>A list of the tax payments you already made this year, and last year&rsquo;s return (which shows the figure you can base this year&rsquo;s payments on to avoid a penalty)</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Income</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every payment you actually received during the year, from your own records. An invoice you sent but haven&rsquo;t been paid for is not income yet, and the 1099s you received will miss anything under the reporting thresholds.</li>\n<li>Any 1099-NEC, 1099-MISC or 1099-K received.</li>\n<li>Fee reports from anything that processed payments for you (e.g. Stripe, PayPal, Square, Etsy, Upwork). This is because a $1,000 sale arrives as about $971 and the tax return needs the $1,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Note on that final point</h4>\n<p>&ldquo;Anything that processed payments for you&rdquo; can be broken into three groups, and they behave differently:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Card processors:</b> Stripe, Square, PayPal, Helcim. You use these to take card payments from clients.</li>\n<li><b>Peer-to-peer apps:</b> Venmo, Cash App. Only the business profiles count. A friend sending you $40 for dinner on personal Venmo isn&rsquo;t this.</li>\n<li><b>Marketplaces that pay you:</b> Etsy, Upwork, Fiverr, Uber, DoorDash, Amazon. They collect from the customer, take a cut, and pass on the rest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Expenses</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Expenses sorted into Schedule C categories</li>\n<li>Mileage log with dates, given the mid-year rate change</li>\n<li>Home office square footage, plus home expense totals if using the regular method</li>\n<li>Health insurance premiums paid</li>\n<li>Retirement contributions</li>\n<li>Records of equipment purchases, with dates and amounts</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Everything else</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Each estimated payment made, federal and state, with dates and amounts</li>\n<li>Last year&rsquo;s return: it holds the total tax you owed (which sets your payment target for this year) and any unused deductions or losses that carry into this year.</li>\n<li>W-2s and other household income</li>\n<li>Bank details for a refund or payment</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Goes deeper:</b> Getting ready for tax season &mdash; the prep checklist</div></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="deadlines">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">03</div><h2>Deadlines And How To Pay</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">Every date in the tax year, what each one requires, and the specific routes money takes to reach the IRS.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="all-deadlines" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Every Deadline In A Self-Employed Tax Year</div>\n<div class="row-s">A self-employed tax year has four dates for paying tax in advance, one date to file your return, a later date if you ask for extra time, and (if you paid contractors) a January date for sending them their 1099 forms.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><div class="figs forms">\n<div><span>January 15, 2026</span><span>Q4 estimated payment for tax year 2025</span></div>\n<div><span>January 31, 2026</span><span>1099-NECs due to contractors you paid in 2025</span></div>\n<div><span>April 15, 2026</span><span>2025 return due &middot; balance due &middot; Q1 2026 estimated payment</span></div>\n<div><span>June 15, 2026</span><span>Q2 2026 estimated payment</span></div>\n<div><span>September 15, 2026</span><span>Q3 2026 estimated payment</span></div>\n<div><span>October 15, 2026</span><span>Extended filing deadline for 2025 returns</span></div>\n<div><span>January 15, 2027</span><span>Q4 2026 estimated payment</span></div>\n<div><span>April 15, 2027</span><span>2026 return due</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>April 15 does double duty: It is the filing deadline for the year just ended <em>and</em> the first estimated payment for the year already underway. Two different payments for two different tax years leave on the same day, and mislabeling them is a common cause of misapplied payments.</p>\n<p>The estimated tax periods are not equal in length. The second covers two months and the third covers three. Deadlines falling on a weekend or holiday move to the next business day.</p>\n<p>If your state has an income tax, everything above happens twice. You make a second set of quarterly payments and file a second return, using the state&rsquo;s own forms and its own website. The due dates usually match the federal ones, but paying the IRS does not pay your state.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="safe-harbor" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">The Safe Harbor Rule: Avoiding A Penalty Without Predicting Your Year</div>\n<div class="row-s">You&rsquo;re supposed to pay tax four times during the year. But you don&rsquo;t know what you&rsquo;ll owe for the year until the year is over. So what do you send in April?</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>There are two safe amount options. You won&rsquo;t be penalized as long as you hit either one across your four payments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>90% of what you end up owing this year</b>, or</li>\n<li><b>100% of your total tax from last year.</b> Or, 110% if last year&rsquo;s adjusted gross income was over $150,000 ($75,000 if married filing separately)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class="fine">Note: Total tax is a line on last year&rsquo;s return, not the amount you paid in April.</p>\n<p>The first option requires you to accurately predict your income for the year (which isn&rsquo;t always possible). The second option doesn&rsquo;t, as you already have that number on last year&rsquo;s return.</p>\n<h4>What the Safe Harbor Rule protects you from</h4>\n<p>Say you owed $8,000 last year. You pay $2,000 a quarter, hitting $8,000. Then this year goes unusually well and you actually owe $20,000.</p>\n<p>You still owe the extra $12,000, due April 15. But you owe no penalty, because you paid the safe harbor amount.</p>\n<p>One catch: the prior-year option needs a prior year. In your first year there&rsquo;s no figure to use, which is exactly why it&rsquo;s more important to stay on top of this during the year.</p>\n<p>Estimated payments are generally required when at least $1,000 is expected to be owed after withholding and credits.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="how-to-pay" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">How To Send Your Tax Payment To The IRS</div>\n<div class="row-s">You can pay straight from your bank account with IRS Direct Pay, through EFTPS (the government&rsquo;s free online payment system), by debit or credit card, or by mailing a check with a Form 1040-ES slip. Every payment has to say which tax year it is for and why you are paying.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><h4>Federal options</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>IRS Direct Pay:</b> This option pays from a checking or savings account with no fee and no registration. It&rsquo;s the most common route for estimated payments.</li>\n<li><b>EFTPS (Electronic Federal Tax Payment System):</b> This requires enrollment, which takes several days as a PIN arrives by post, but it allows scheduling payments in advance.</li>\n<li><b>Your IRS online account:</b> Pay and also see payment history, balances and notices.</li>\n<li><b>Debit or credit card:</b> You can do this through approved processors, which charge a fee.</li>\n<li><b>Check by post:</b> Send with a Form 1040-ES voucher, to the address in the instructions, which varies by state.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="caution"><b>Label every payment.</b> Each payment has to specify the tax year it applies to and the reason, such as estimated tax, balance due, or extension. An April payment intended as a Q1 estimate for the current year, applied instead to last year&rsquo;s balance, produces a penalty notice months later. Direct Pay asks for both explicitly.</div>\n<p>Keep a record of every payment with its date, amount and confirmation number. That log is needed when preparing the return, and it is the only practical way to reconcile against an IRS notice.</p>\n<p>State payments go through the state&rsquo;s own system, separately. Paying the IRS does not pay the state.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="extensions" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">An Extension To File Is NOT An Extension To Pay</div>\n<div class="row-s">Form 4868 gives you until October to send in the paperwork. However, that extra time is only for the paperwork. Any tax money you owe is still due in April, and unpaid tax starts building up interest from that date.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>The automatic six-month extension is easy to obtain and requires no explanation. It extends only the deadline for submitting the return.</p>\n<p>Tax money owed is still due in April. An extension filed without payment means interest and failure-to-pay charges run from April regardless.</p>\n<p>The usual approach where information is genuinely missing is to estimate the tax owed, pay that amount with the extension, and file the accurate return later. If you pay too much, you get the extra back. If you pay too little, you&rsquo;re only charged on the part you didn&rsquo;t pay. An estimate on the high side costs less than one on the low side.</p>\n<p>Note: Extensions are routine and do not increase audit risk.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="missed-payments" data-t="behind">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">What Happens If You Miss A Payment Or Spend The Tax Money</div>\n<div class="row-s">If you pay less during the year than you should have, the IRS charges something that works like interest. It&rsquo;s about 7% a year as of 2026, on the amount you were short, for the days it went unpaid. However, not filing your return is punished far more heavily than not paying.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><div class="figs">\n<div><span>Underpayment rate, Q3 2026</span><span>7%</span></div>\n<div><span>Rate in Q2 2026</span><span>6%</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>The rate resets quarterly. Because it is annualized, a payment a few weeks late costs a fraction of the headline figure. Missing a quarterly payment is not a filing failure and carries no separate late-filing consequence.</p>\n<p>Form 2210 offers an annualized income method, which recognizes that income arrived unevenly. It takes more work but can reduce or remove the charge where earnings were concentrated late in the year.</p>\n<h4>When the money is already gone</h4>\n<p>Even if you can&rsquo;t pay in full, still file the return and pay whatever you&rsquo;re able to pay. This is because the failure-to-file penalty runs at a substantially higher monthly rate than the failure-to-pay penalty. Filing on time without full payment removes the larger problem immediately.</p>\n<p>If you can&rsquo;t pay the full amount, here are a few options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Short-term payment plans</b> for balances clearable within a few months</li>\n<li><b>Long-term installment agreements</b> spreading the balance over years, applied for online</li>\n<li><b>First-time penalty abatement</b>, available to people with a previously clean compliance history</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You&rsquo;ll still accrue interest under a payment plan, but considerably more slowly than under no arrangement at all.</p>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Goes deeper:</b> You spent your tax money. Here&rsquo;s how to recover.</div></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="income">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">04</div><h2>What Counts As Taxable Income</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">Nearly all of it. The forms you receive don&rsquo;t define what&rsquo;s taxable &mdash; and two thresholds moved sharply for 2026.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="no-form-still-income" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Income Counts Whether Or Not A Form Arrives <span class="tag">2026 change</span></div>\n<div class="row-s">A 1099 is a copy of information sent to you and to the IRS. A 1099 only reports income that already exists &mdash; it does not decide what counts as income. Money you earn without ever getting a 1099 is taxed just like money that came with one.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>This matters more in 2026 than in past years, because both reporting thresholds rose:</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>1099-NEC, 2026 (was $600)</td><td>$2,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1099-K from a payment app or marketplace</td><td>$20,000 and 200+ transactions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1099-K for card payments</td><td>No minimum &mdash; any amount</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Someone paid $1,800 by a client in 2026 will often receive nothing, where in 2025 a 1099-NEC would have arrived. The $1,800 is still income and still belongs on Schedule C.</p>\n<p>The practical consequence: your own records are now the primary source, and the forms are an incomplete cross-check by design.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="nec-vs-k" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">How Two Different 1099 Forms Can Report The Same Payment</div>\n<div class="row-s">A 1099-NEC comes from a client who paid you for work. A 1099-K comes from a payment app or card processor reporting money that passed through it. If the same payment lands on both forms, adding both totals counts that money twice.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p><b>1099-NEC</b> &mdash; sent by a business that paid you $2,000 or more for services in 2026.</p>\n<p><b>1099-K</b> &mdash; sent by a payment processor or platform, reporting gross volume before fees.</p>\n<p>Two different thresholds hide inside that one form, and which applies depends on how the customer paid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Paid you by credit or debit card.</b> The card processor reports it with no minimum at all. Take $500 of card payments and a 1099-K still arrives.</li>\n<li><b>Paid you through an app or marketplace</b> from a balance or bank account. Only reported above $20,000 <em>and</em> 200 transactions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some services do both, so the same provider can report one payment and not another. Either way the income is taxable, and either way your own records are what you file from.</p>\n<h4>Three things that go wrong</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Double counting.</b> A client paying through a platform may issue a 1099-NEC while the platform issues a 1099-K covering the same payment.</li>\n<li><b>Gross versus net.</b> A 1099-K reports the amount before platform fees. A $1,000 sale that netted $971 still shows as $1,000; the $29 belongs on the expense side, not as quietly reduced revenue.</li>\n<li><b>Personal transfers.</b> Money from friends isn&rsquo;t income, but a platform may include it in gross volume if the account is set up for business. Separate accounts prevent this.</li>\n</ul></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="cash-basis" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Income Counts When It Arrives, Not When You Invoice</div>\n<div class="row-s">Most self-employed people use cash-basis accounting. A December invoice paid in January is January&rsquo;s income, and an invoice never paid is not income at all.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>Cash basis counts money when it moves: income when you get paid, expenses when you pay. Accrual basis counts money earlier. Income is counted when you finish the work, and expenses are counted when you receive the bill, regardless of whether any money has changed hands. Most self-employed people use cash basis, and you mark which one you use on Schedule C.</p>\n<p>On cash basis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A check received December 30 is generally December income even if deposited in January, because it was available to you.</li>\n<li>An unpaid invoice is neither income nor a deduction &mdash; nothing was counted, so there is nothing to write off.</li>\n<li>An expense charged to a credit card in December counts in December, even if the card is paid in January.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is why the end of December matters: which side of the line money lands on decides which year it belongs to.</p></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="calculation">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">05</div><h2>How Your Tax Bill Is Calculated</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">First, we discuss what the standard deduction is, how brackets work, and what the 20% business deduction does. Then, you&rsquo;ll learn how all of these pieces are assembled into one bill.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="standard-deduction" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">What The Standard Deduction Is</div>\n<div class="row-s">The standard deduction is a fixed amount the government lets everyone subtract from their income before income tax is calculated. You do not have to spend anything or keep any receipts to get the standard deduction.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>It exists so the first slice of what you earn is not taxed at all. Everyone gets it, employees and self-employed people alike, and the amount depends only on your filing status.</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Single</td><td>$16,100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Married filing jointly</td><td>$32,200</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Head of household</td><td>$24,150</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class="fine">2026 amounts. They rise most years.</p>\n<h4>What it does to your bill</h4>\n<p>A single person with $50,000 of income does not pay income tax on $50,000. They subtract $16,100 first and pay income tax on what is left.</p>\n<div class="eq">\n<div>$50,000 &minus; $16,100 = <b>$33,900 subject to income tax</b></div>\n</div>\n<p>Someone who earns less than the standard deduction generally owes no income tax at all, because there is nothing left after it comes off.</p>\n<h4>Two things it does not do</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>It does not reduce self-employment tax.</b> That tax is calculated separately and the standard deduction never touches it. This is why someone can owe no income tax at all and still get a tax bill. Below is an example, using $16,000 for the year:\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Employee earning $16,000</th><th>Self-employed, $16,000 profit</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Income tax</td><td>$0</td><td>$0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Social Security + Medicare</td><td>$1,224 from their paychecks<br>(employer pays another $1,224)</td><td>$2,261</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Already taken out?</td><td>Yes, every paycheck</td><td>No, you write the check</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The employee is not tax-free either. They pay 7.65% of their wages in Social Security and Medicare, and no standard deduction shields them from it. It just came out invisibly, and their employer quietly paid a matching $1,224 they never saw. The self-employed bill is nearly double, and nothing has been set aside for it.</p></li>\n<li><b>It is not the same as your business expenses.</b> Business expenses are subtracted BEFORE the standard deduction is applied. So if you are single and made $20,000 before expenses but $15,000 after expenses, you would fall below the $16,100 standard deduction and owe no income tax. You would still owe self-employment tax on the $15,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Itemizing: the alternative almost nobody uses</h4>\n<p>Instead of the standard deduction you can add up certain personal expenses, like mortgage interest, state taxes, large medical bills, and charitable donations, and subtract those instead. That is called <b>itemizing</b>, and you take one or the other, never both.</p>\n<p>Roughly 90% of people take the standard deduction, because their itemizable expenses add up to less than the flat amount. Tax software works out which is bigger for you automatically.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="brackets" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">How Tax Brackets Work</div>\n<div class="row-s">Tax brackets apply to chunks of income, not to all of it. A single person in the 22% bracket pays 10% on their first $12,400, 12% on the next chunk, and 22% only on income above $50,400.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>Once the standard deduction has come off, whatever is left runs through the brackets. Each chunk of income is taxed at its own rate.</p>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>10%</span><span>$0 &ndash; $12,400</span></div>\n<div><span>12%</span><span>above $12,400</span></div>\n<div><span>22%</span><span>above $50,400</span></div>\n<div><span>24%</span><span>above $105,700</span></div>\n<div><span>32%</span><span>above $201,775</span></div>\n<div><span>35%</span><span>above $256,225</span></div>\n<div><span>37%</span><span>above $640,600</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class="fine">2026 brackets, single filers, applied to income after deductions.</p>\n<h4>What that looks like</h4>\n<p>Someone with $24,294 left after deductions does not pay one single rate on it. It gets split:</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>First $12,400, taxed at 10%</td><td>$1,240</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Remaining $11,894, taxed at 12%</td><td>$1,427</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Income tax owed</td><td>$2,667</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Marginal rate</b> is the rate on your next dollar earned. In the example above, 12% is the marginal rate, because the next dollar they earn is taxed at 12%.</p>\n<p><b>Effective rate</b> is the tax divided by the income it came from:</p>\n<div class="eq">\n<div>$2,667 &divide; $50,000 = <b>5.3%</b></div>\n</div>\n<p>So that person is in the 12% bracket, but income tax actually costs them 5.3% of what they made.</p>\n<p class="fine">That 5.3% is income tax only. Self-employment tax is a separate bill on the same $50,000, and the last row in this section adds the two together.</p>\n<p>This is why turning down work to stay out of a higher bracket does not hold up. Only the portion above the threshold is taxed at the higher rate, so more gross income still leaves more after tax.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="qbi" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">The 20% Business Deduction (Qualified Business Income)</div>\n<div class="row-s">On top of the standard deduction, most self-employed people can subtract another 20% of their business profit before income tax is calculated. Its official name is the qualified business income deduction.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>It arrived with the 2017 tax law. Corporations got a rate cut, and this was the version for businesses like yours that do not pay corporate rates. The 2025 legislation made it permanent.</p>\n<h4>The catch on the 20%</h4>\n<p>It is not simply 20% of your profit. There is a ceiling: the deduction cannot exceed <b>20% of what is left after your standard deduction</b>. You get whichever number is smaller.</p>\n<p>For a single person with $50,000 of profit:</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp ann">\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>20% of business profit</td><td>$50,000 &minus; $3,532 (half your self-employment tax) = $46,468<br>$46,468 &times; 20%</td><td>$9,294</td></tr>\n<tr><td>20% of income after the standard deduction</td><td>$46,468 &minus; $16,100 (standard deduction) = $30,368<br>$30,368 &times; 20%</td><td>$6,074</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Deduction you get, the smaller one</td><td></td><td>$6,074</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>At higher incomes the first number usually wins. At lower incomes the ceiling binds, which is why the deduction often comes out smaller than the headline 20% suggests.</p>\n<h4>Income limits</h4>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>Threshold &mdash; single</span><span>$201,750</span></div>\n<div><span>Fully phased in by</span><span>$276,750</span></div>\n<div><span>Threshold &mdash; married filing jointly</span><span>$403,500</span></div>\n<div><span>Fully phased in by</span><span>$553,500</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>Above those thresholds the rules become considerably more complex. Limits based on wages paid and property owned begin to apply, and certain service businesses, such as health, law, accounting, consulting, performing arts, athletics, financial services, and any business trading mainly on the reputation or skill of its owner, lose the deduction entirely once fully phased out.</p>\n<p>It does not reduce self-employment tax, and does not apply to investment income, capital gains or wages.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="two-taxes" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Putting It Together: The Two Taxes You Owe</div>\n<div class="row-s">You owe two separate taxes on the same business profit: self-employment tax and income tax. Each is calculated a different way, which is why knowing your tax bracket alone does not tell you what you&rsquo;ll actually pay.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><div class="eq">\n<div>Your total tax bill = <b>self-employment tax</b> + <b>income tax</b></div>\n</div>\n<p><b>Self-employment tax</b> pays for Social Security (12.4%) and Medicare (2.9%). It is a flat 15.3%.</p>\n<p><b>Income tax</b> is the regular federal tax everyone pays, using the tax brackets discussed above.</p>\n<p>Both calculations start from your net profit, which is revenue minus business expenses. But each one shrinks that number in its own way before applying its rate.</p>\n<p>The example below uses $50,000 of net profit for a single person with no other income.</p>\n\n<h4>Tax 1: Self-employment tax</h4>\n<p>At a job, employees pay half of Social Security and Medicare and the employer pays the other half. Working for yourself, you pay both halves.</p>\n<div class="eq">\n<div>15.3% = 12.4% Social Security + 2.9% Medicare</div>\n</div>\n<p>Before the total 15.3% is applied, 7.65% of your profit is taken out, leaving 92.35% to be taxed. This is what keeps self-employed people taxed on roughly the same base as employees.</p>\n<div class="eq">\n<div>Profit that gets taxed: $50,000 &times; 92.35% = <b>$46,175</b></div>\n<div>Self-employment tax: $46,175 &times; 15.3% = <b>$7,065</b></div>\n</div>\n<p>7.65% is exactly what an employer pays. Here is the same $100 of work, done two ways:</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>An employee</th><th>Self-employed</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Gets paid</td><td>$100</td><td>$100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Employer pays on top</td><td>$7.65</td><td>$0 &mdash; you are also the employer</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Taxed on</td><td>$100</td><td>$92.35</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The employee is taxed on $100, and their employer&rsquo;s $7.65 sits outside that &mdash; it was never the employee&rsquo;s money. Your $100 has to cover both sides, so the law takes 7.65% out to match.</p>\n<p>Nothing else affects this calculation. The standard deduction and the 20% business deduction leave self-employment tax completely alone.</p>\n\n<h4>Tax 2: Income tax</h4>\n<p>Here the deductions from the rows above come off first:</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp ann">\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Net profit</td><td>Revenue minus expenses</td><td>$50,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minus half your self-employment tax</td><td>Half of the $7,065 above</td><td>&minus;$3,532</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minus the standard deduction</td><td>Set by the government</td><td>&minus;$16,100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minus the 20% business deduction</td><td>20% of $30,368, the capped figure from the row above</td><td>&minus;$6,074</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Taxed on</td><td></td><td>$24,294</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Run through the brackets, $24,294 comes to <b>$2,667</b>.</p>\n\n<h4>The two side by side</h4>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Self-employment tax</th><th>Income tax</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Starts from</td><td>$50,000</td><td>$50,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What comes off first</td><td>7.65%</td><td>$25,706 of deductions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Amount actually taxed</td><td>$46,175</td><td>$24,294</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rate applied</td><td>15.3%</td><td>10% and 12% brackets</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>You owe</td><td>$7,065</td><td>$2,667</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class="eq">\n<div>$7,065 + $2,667 = <b>$9,732</b></div>\n<div><span>about 19.5% of your $50,000 profit, both taxes together</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class="fine">Income tax on its own was 5.3% of that same profit, as worked out in the brackets row above. Self-employment tax is what takes the combined figure to 19.5%.</p>\n<p>Subtract any credits and anything you already paid during the year, and what is left is your balance due or refund.</p>\n<p>A 12% bracket alone would suggest far less than 19.5%. Adding 12% and 15.3% would suggest 27%, far more. Neither shortcut works, which is why both calculations have to be run separately.</p>\n<p class="fine">Illustration only: single filer, no other income, ignoring state tax and credits.</p></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="deductions">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">06</div><h2>What Counts As A Business Deduction</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">Tax is owed on profit, not on what you were paid. But whether a given purchase reduces that profit is more conditional than most guides suggest, so this starts with how the question is decided.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="no-official-list" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">There Is No Official List Of Deductible Things</div>\n<div class="row-s">There is no official list of what you can write off. Whether a purchase is deductible depends on the details of your own work, so the same item can be deductible for one person and not for another.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>The statutory standard is that an expense must be <b>ordinary</b>, defined as common and accepted in your particular line of work, and <b>necessary</b>, meaning helpful and appropriate for it. Neither word has a fixed dollar figure or a published list attached.</p>\n<p>Three conditions generally have to hold before an expense reduces taxable profit:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>It fits your trade or business.</b> A camera is ordinary for a photographer and harder to justify for a bookkeeper.</li>\n<li><b>The business portion is separated from the personal portion.</b> Purchases used for both work and personal life are allocated, and the allocation has to be reasonable and consistently applied. For example, if your $1,200 phone bill is about 60% business use, you deduct $720. The 60% needs a basis &mdash; say you tracked calls for a week and found it was roughly 60% &mdash; and it should stay 60% next year unless your work actually changed.</li>\n<li><b>It can be substantiated.</b> For most expenses a receipt is enough. Travel, meals and vehicles need one extra thing: a short note on who it was with and what the business reason was. A $60 restaurant receipt shows the amount and the date, but not that you were there with a client discussing a contract, so write that down.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A fourth issue applies to larger purchases: some costs cannot simply be deducted in the year they are paid. A $4,000 camera technically has to be spread over about five years. However, an election on your return usually lets you deduct the whole purchase now. Anything under $2,500 per item skips the question entirely.</p>\n<div class="caution"><b>How to read the rest of this section.</b> The categories below are where business expenses commonly fall, and the conditions attached to each are the ones that most often decide the answer. They are not a list of things that are automatically deductible. Whether any specific purchase qualifies depends on facts a general guide cannot know.</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="categories" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">The Categories Most Business Expenses Fall Into</div>\n<div class="row-s">Most business expenses fall into a few groups: equipment, software, professional fees, contractor payments, advertising, insurance, and the licenses, memberships and training that keep you qualified in the work you already do. Each one only counts if it is normal for your kind of work, split away from personal use, and backed up by records.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><h4>Commonly claimed categories</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Equipment and technology</b> like computers, phones, cameras, and tools. Personal use is allocated out. Larger purchases may need to be capitalized, meaning the deduction is spread across several years rather than taken in full in year one.</li>\n<li><b>Software and subscriptions</b> used for the work.</li>\n<li><b>Professional services</b> such as accounting, legal and consulting fees connected to the business.</li>\n<li><b>Contractor payments</b>, which may create a reporting obligation for you; see <a href="#issuing-1099s">Forms you have to send out</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Advertising and marketing</b> like ad spend, website hosting, and design.</li>\n<li><b>Business insurance.</b></li>\n<li><b>Bank and payment processing fees</b> on business accounts.</li>\n<li><b>Licenses, professional memberships</b> and education that maintains or improves skills in your <em>current</em> field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Where people commonly get it wrong</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Clothing.</b> The test is whether the item is suitable for everyday wear, not whether it was bought for work. Ordinary clothes generally do not qualify even if only ever worn for business.</li>\n<li><b>Education.</b> Training that qualifies you for a new trade or profession is treated differently from training that improves your existing one.</li>\n<li><b>Commuting.</b> Travel between home and a regular workplace is personal, not business.</li>\n<li><b>Justifying a purchase afterwards.</b> The business purpose needs to be real at the time, not constructed at filing.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Goes deeper:</b> What self-employed people can actually deduct, category by category</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="home-office" data-t="home">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">The Home Office Deduction: Who Qualifies, And Two Ways To Figure It</div>\n<div class="row-s">A home office only counts if you use that room or area regularly and <em>only</em> for business. The word &ldquo;only&rdquo; is what rules out most setups. For a space that does qualify, the simple method pays $5 per square foot up to $1,500, and the detailed method has no limit but needs far more record-keeping.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>Exclusively is the condition that decides most cases. A kitchen table used for work by day and dinner by night generally does not qualify. A spare room used only for work generally does. There is no partial credit for a corner of a room used for both.</p>\n<p>The space must also be the principal place of business, or a place used regularly to meet clients.</p>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>Simplified rate</span><span>$5 per sq ft</span></div>\n<div><span>Maximum square footage</span><span>300</span></div>\n<div><span>Maximum deduction</span><span>$1,500</span></div>\n</div>\n<h4>Simplified method = square footage &times; $5</h4>\n<p>With this method, you never need to know what you paid for rent, utilities or repairs. The flat rate covers all of it.</p>\n<p>If you own your home, this method doesn&rsquo;t depreciate any of your home&rsquo;s value, which matters later. The regular method does, and that depreciation gets taxed when you sell your home, even when the rest of the money earned from the sale isn&rsquo;t taxed.</p>\n<p>One limit is that a home office deduction cannot create a loss. It is capped at the profit from the business run out of that space, worked out before the home office deduction itself. If that profit is $1,000, the most you can deduct is $1,000, not the full $1,500, and under the simplified method the remaining $500 is forfeited rather than rolling into next year.</p>\n<h4>Regular method</h4>\n<p>Calculate the office as a percentage of the home, then deduct that percentage of rent or mortgage interest, utilities, insurance and repairs. With this method there is no dollar cap, and unused amounts can carry forward. Homeowners must depreciate the office portion, which creates a recapture calculation on sale. Reported on Form 8829.</p>\n<p>Methods can be switched between years, but not within a year.</p>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Goes deeper:</b> The home office deduction, both methods worked through</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="mileage" data-t="drive">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">The 2026 Mileage Rate Changed Halfway Through The Year <span class="tag">2026 change</span></div>\n<div class="row-s">For business driving in 2026 you can deduct 72.5 cents per mile from January through June, and 76 cents per mile from July through December. Work driving also has stricter record-keeping rules than most other expenses.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><div class="figs">\n<div><span>Jan 1 &ndash; Jun 30, 2026</span><span>72.5&cent; per mile</span></div>\n<div><span>Jul 1 &ndash; Dec 31, 2026</span><span>76&cent; per mile</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>A single annual mileage total cannot be converted correctly. The log needs dates.</p>\n<h4>Which miles qualify</h4>\n<p>Travel between work locations, to clients, to collect supplies, or to a business meeting can generally count as a deduction. The regular commute between home and a regular workplace does not count.</p>\n<p>Where a qualifying home office is the principal place of business, trips from there to a client can be business miles rather than commuting, which is one reason the home office test matters beyond the deduction itself.</p>\n<h4>Standard rate or actual expenses</h4>\n<p>The alternative to the standard mileage rate is deducting the business-use percentage of what the vehicle actually costs. For example, if gas, insurance, repairs and registration came to $6,000 and about 60% of the miles driven were for business, you deduct $3,600. This method can produce a higher figure for expensive vehicles or vehicles with high running costs, but it requires considerably more record-keeping.</p>\n<p>The choice matters most in the first year, and it locks in one direction. If you claim depreciation on the vehicle under the actual expense method in year one, you generally cannot switch to the standard mileage rate for that vehicle later. Starting with the standard mileage rate leaves more room to change your mind.</p>\n<div class="caution">Vehicles are subject to heightened substantiation rules. Keep a log with the date, mileage and business purpose for each trip, written as you go. Reconstructed logs are a common problem in examinations.</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="meals-travel" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Business Meals Are Generally 50% Deductible and Travel Is Generally 100%</div>\n<div class="row-s">For a meal with a client, or one you eat while traveling for work, you can normally deduct only half of what the meal cost. Flights, hotels and ground transportation on a business trip can normally be deducted in full.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>The 50% limit applies whether the meal is with a client or eaten alone while traveling. Tips are included before the 50% limit is applied.</p>\n<p>Travel costs are generally deductible in full when the primary purpose of the trip is business. Travel costs often include flights, trains, rental cars, hotels, rideshares to business destinations, parking, tolls, baggage fees, and conference registration.</p>\n<h4>Mixed trips</h4>\n<p>A trip can be extended for personal time, but only the business portion is deductible. Three conference days followed by two holiday days means three days of lodging and meals, not five. The flight itself can still be deductible where the trip&rsquo;s primary purpose was business.</p>\n<div class="caution">Meals and travel carry heightened substantiation requirements. Records generally need the amount, the date, the place, and the business purpose. For meals with others, you also record who was there and the business relationship. A card statement alone is usually not enough to show an expense was for business.</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="startup-costs" data-t="first">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Startup Costs From Before The Business Opened</div>\n<div class="row-s">You can deduct up to $5,000 of the money you spent getting the business started, plus another $5,000 of the legal and filing costs of setting it up. Anything above those two $5,000 limits gets deducted a little at a time over 15 years.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><div class="figs">\n<div><span>First-year startup cost deduction</span><span>up to $5,000</span></div>\n<div><span>First-year organizational cost deduction</span><span>up to $5,000</span></div>\n<div><span>Reduced dollar-for-dollar above</span><span>$50,000</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>Startup costs are those incurred investigating or setting up the business before it began operating. Some examples are market research, travel to find suppliers, pre-opening advertising, training, and related professional fees. Organizational costs are the legal and filing costs of forming an entity.</p>\n<p>The $5,000 shrinks by one dollar for every dollar your total startup costs go over $50,000, and disappears at $55,000. Spend $52,000 and you deduct $3,000; spend $55,000 and you deduct nothing in year one. Whatever is not deducted now is spread over 15 years instead. Most self-employed people never come close to $50,000, so this rarely comes up.</p>\n<p>The trigger is when the business became active, not when it became profitable.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="health-premiums" data-t="ownins">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Health Insurance Premiums Reduce Income Tax But Not Self-Employment Tax</div>\n<div class="row-s">Self-employed people can subtract health, dental and long-term care premiums from their income before income tax is figured, even without itemizing. But you cannot deduct more than your business profit, you cannot deduct for any month you could have joined a plan through your own or a spouse&rsquo;s job, and it lowers income tax only. It never lowers self-employment tax.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>This is an above-the-line deduction, so it works whether or not you itemize. It goes on Schedule 1 and is calculated on Form 7206.</p>\n<h4>The three limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Capped at net profit.</b> The deduction for health insurance premiums cannot exceed earned income from the business the plan is established under. For example, a business with $4,000 of profit cannot deduct $9,000 of premiums.</li>\n<li><b>Disqualified by employer coverage.</b> You cannot deduct premiums for any month you were <em>eligible</em> for a subsidized plan through your own or a spouse&rsquo;s employer. Being eligible disqualifies you even if you declined to enroll.</li>\n<li><b>Income tax only.</b> Unlike most business deductions, this one does not reduce net earnings for self-employment tax.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Premiums for you, a spouse, dependents and children under 27 can generally be included.</p>\n<div class="callout">\n<div class="k">Worth knowing for 2026</div>\n<p>The enhanced marketplace subsidies that ran from 2021 through 2025 expired on December 31, 2025. For 2026 the premium tax credit runs between 100% and 400% of the federal poverty line, and above 400% it drops to zero rather than phasing down. Income level therefore affects marketplace costs far more sharply than last year.</p>\n<p>Legislation to extend the enhanced subsidies was still moving through Congress in early 2026 and had not been enacted as of August 2026.</p>\n</div>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Goes deeper:</b> Health insurance for the self employed &mdash; the dedicated guide</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="hobby-loss" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">When The IRS May Treat Your Business As A Hobby</div>\n<div class="row-s">You can only deduct business expenses if you are genuinely trying to make money. Work that loses money year after year may be treated as a hobby instead of a business, which sharply limits what you can write off.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>This sits underneath everything else in this section. If an activity is not carried on for profit, the deduction rules that apply to businesses do not apply to it.</p>\n<p>There is no single test. The IRS weighs a set of factors, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Whether the activity is carried on in a businesslike way, with proper books</li>\n<li>The time and effort put in</li>\n<li>Whether losses are ordinary for a business in its start-up phase</li>\n<li>Whether methods have been changed in an attempt to improve profitability</li>\n<li>The taxpayer&rsquo;s expertise, and any history of making similar activities profitable</li>\n<li>Whether there are elements of personal pleasure or recreation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A commonly cited guideline is that an activity showing a profit in at least three of the last five years is generally presumed to be for profit. However, not being profitable in three of the last five years does not automatically disqualify you either.</p>\n<p>Genuine early-stage losses are ordinary and expected. The concern arises with sustained losses in activities that also have a personal or recreational dimension.</p></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="credits">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">07</div><h2>Which Tax Credits You Can Claim</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">A credit is money taken off the tax bill itself. A deduction only reduces the income the bill is calculated from, making a credit worth considerably more.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="credit-vs-deduction" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">A Credit Comes Off The Tax. A Deduction Comes Off The Income</div>\n<div class="row-s">A deduction and a credit both sound like $2,000 off, but they aren&rsquo;t. A deduction only reduces the income your tax is calculated from. A credit comes off the tax bill itself.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>A <b>deduction</b> comes off your income before tax is calculated. For example, a $2,000 deduction at a tax rate of 12% only saves you $240.</p>\n<p>A <b>credit</b> comes off the tax bill itself, after it has been worked out. For example, a $2,000 credit saves you $2,000, at any income level.</p>\n<p>Say you owe $5,000 in tax:</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>$2,000 deduction</th><th>$2,000 credit</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Tax bill before</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$5,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What the $2,000 does</td><td>Removes $2,000 from your taxable income</td><td>Comes straight off the tax bill</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You save</td><td>$240 (12% of $2,000)</td><td>$2,000</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Tax bill after</td><td>$4,760</td><td>$3,000</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Three kinds of tax credits</h4>\n<p>These three words answer one question: what happens if your credit is bigger than your tax bill? Say you owe $500 in tax and have a $2,000 credit.</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Nonrefundable</th><th>Refundable</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Tax owed</td><td>$500</td><td>$500</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Your credit</td><td>$2,000</td><td>$2,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tax bill after</td><td>$0</td><td>$0</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Money sent to you</td><td>nothing</td><td>$1,500</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <b>nonrefundable</b> credit stops when your bill hits zero. The leftover $1,500 just disappears and you never see it.</p>\n<p>A <b>refundable</b> credit means the government actually pays you the difference. Your bill hits zero and the remaining $1,500 comes back to you in cash.</p>\n<p><b>Partially refundable</b> means part of the credit behaves each way. The Child Tax Credit is $2,200 per child, of which $1,700 is refundable. Therefore, up to $1,700 can come back to you as money, while the other $500 can only cancel out tax you actually owed.</p>\n<p>This matters most in low-income years, which are common in self-employment. In a year when you owe little or no tax, a nonrefundable credit is worth little or nothing, while a refundable one still pays out.</p>\n<p>Credits are claimed on the return, and most require a specific form attached. Nearly all have income limits, and most phase out gradually rather than cutting off.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="child-credits" data-t="kids">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">The Child Tax Credit And The Child And Dependent Care Credit</div>\n<div class="row-s">The Child Tax Credit is $2,200 per child for 2026, and $1,700 of that can come back to you as a refund even if you owe no tax. For 2026 the Child and Dependent Care Credit can pay back up to 50% of what you spent on childcare, up from 35%.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><div class="figs">\n<div><span>Child Tax Credit per qualifying child</span><span>$2,200</span></div>\n<div><span>&hellip; refundable portion</span><span>$1,700</span></div>\n<div><span>Care credit &mdash; max expenses, one person</span><span>$3,000</span></div>\n<div><span>Care credit &mdash; max expenses, two or more</span><span>$6,000</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>The Child Tax Credit applies to qualifying children under 17 and phases out at higher incomes.</p>\n<p>The Child and Dependent Care Credit covers care that allowed you to work, such as daycare, after-school care, and day camp. Self-employed work counts as work for this purpose, so it is available on the same basis as it is to employees. Claimed on Form 2441. The maximum credit rate rose from 35% to 50% for 2026.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="eitc-savers" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">The Earned Income Credit And The Saver&rsquo;s Credit</div>\n<div class="row-s">Many self-employed people think they cannot get either the Earned Income Credit or the Saver&rsquo;s Credit. However, business profit does count as earned income for the Earned Income Tax Credit, and the Saver&rsquo;s Credit pays back part of what you put into a retirement account.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><h4>Earned Income Tax Credit</h4>\n<p>Fully refundable. Net self-employment profit counts as earned income for it.</p>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>No qualifying children</span><span>$664</span></div>\n<div><span>One child</span><span>$4,427</span></div>\n<div><span>Two children</span><span>$7,316</span></div>\n<div><span>Three or more</span><span>$8,231</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>Because it runs on profit rather than revenue, business deductions change the income figure used. This can move someone into or out of the range.</p>\n<h4>Saver&rsquo;s Credit</h4>\n<p>A credit worth 50%, 20% or 10% of a retirement contribution, depending on income. It applies to IRAs and to self-employed retirement plans, and is claimed on Form 8880.</p>\n<div class="figs">\n<div><span>50% rate &mdash; single, up to</span><span>$24,250</span></div>\n<div><span>50% rate &mdash; married filing jointly, up to</span><span>$48,500</span></div>\n<div><span>No credit &mdash; single, above</span><span>$40,250</span></div>\n<div><span>No credit &mdash; married filing jointly, above</span><span>$80,500</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>It is nonrefundable, so it only helps where there is tax to offset. It is also among the least claimed credits in the code, largely because people don&rsquo;t know it exists.</p></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="set-aside">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">08</div><h2>How Much Of Your Business Income To Set Aside</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">How to work out the correct percentage, and why moving the money as it arrives matters more than getting that percentage exactly right.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="what-decides" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">What Decides The Percentage To Set Aside</div>\n<div class="row-s">How much of each payment you should set aside for taxes depends on four things: self-employment tax, which income tax rate your business profit falls into, your state&rsquo;s tax, and whether a spouse&rsquo;s job or a second income has already used up the lower tax rates.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>There is no single correct figure, because it depends on circumstances a general guide cannot know. However, the inputs are the same for everyone:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Self-employment tax:</b> roughly 15.3% of net profit, calculated on 92.35% of net profit, with the Social Security portion stopping at $184,500.</li>\n<li><b>Federal income tax:</b> which brackets the profit falls into after the standard deduction and the qualified business income deduction.</li>\n<li><b>State and local income tax:</b> anywhere from nothing to over 10%.</li>\n<li><b>Other income on the return:</b> tax brackets apply to your household&rsquo;s total income, not to each source separately. So the same business profit gets taxed at very different rates depending on what else is on the return. A spouse&rsquo;s salary, or your own W-2 job, fills the lower brackets first, so business profit stacks on top and is taxed at higher rates than it would be on its own.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That last input is the one most often overlooked. The same $40,000 of profit produces very different tax depending on what else appears on the return.</p>\n<p>However, business expenses reduce both taxes, and adjustments like half of self-employment tax and health insurance premiums come off before income tax is figured. Profit is what gets taxed, not revenue.</p>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Goes deeper:</b> How much to set aside every time you get paid</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="per-payment" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Why Setting Aside Per Payment Is Better Than Saving Up Per Quarter</div>\n<div class="row-s">Putting a set share of every payment into a separate account means the tax payment due every three months is already covered. However, if you wait until the due date to set the money aside, you may find you have already spent it.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>The four due dates have nothing to do with when income arrives. Income that comes unevenly, such as a large project in February, a quiet March, and three invoices in April, doesn&rsquo;t line up with a fixed quarterly demand, and by the due date the money may have gone to a slow month.</p>\n<p>Setting aside a percentage of income as it arrives turns an unpredictable quarterly problem into a predictable per-payment one. Common approaches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A second account that receives a fixed percentage and is used for nothing else</li>\n<li>An automatic transfer rule, so no decision is required each time</li>\n<li>Paying the IRS as income arrives. Estimated payments can be made any time, not only on the four dates</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Creating a genuinely separate account is the part that makes the difference. Money in the main account is spendable regardless of what it was mentally labeled.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="also-w2" data-t="w2yes">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Taxes When You Have Both A Regular Job And Self-Employment Income</div>\n<div class="row-s">Money you earn on the side sits on top of your wages, so it is taxed at the highest rate your paycheck already pushed you into. Having more tax taken out of that paycheck can cover the tax on your side income, so you may not need quarterly payments at all.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>Wages fill the lower brackets first, so business profit is taxed on top of them. This is why side income often produces a larger bill than expected. Self-employment tax still applies to your business profit, but your wages count toward the $184,500 Social Security cap first.</p>\n<p>For example, say you make $170,000 in W-2 wages plus $30,000 of self-employment profit. Your wages already used $170,000 of the $184,500 Social Security cap, leaving only $14,500 of room. So only $14,500 of your business earnings pays the Social Security part. Medicare has no cap, so all of it pays that.</p>\n<div class="cmpwrap"><table class="cmp">\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Business profit</td><td>$30,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Net earnings (&times; 92.35%)</td><td>$27,705</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Social Security (12.4%), only on the $14,500 of remaining cap</td><td>$1,798</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Medicare (2.9%), on all $27,705</td><td>$803</td></tr>\n<tr class="tot"><td>Self-employment tax</td><td>$2,601</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class="fine">Someone with the same $30,000 profit and no job would pay about $4,238, because none of their Social Security cap had been used.</p>\n<h4>The withholding option</h4>\n<p>At a job, your employer takes tax out of every paycheck and sends it to the IRS for you. That is called withholding, and self-employment does not have it.</p>\n<p>If you also have a W-2 job, you can use that job&rsquo;s withholding to cover the tax on your side income and skip quarterly payments. You do it by giving your employer a new Form W-4 asking for more tax to be taken out.</p>\n<p>Withholding can also clear interest charges on payments you missed earlier in the year. Here is why:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Four checkpoints, not one finish line.</b> At each deadline &mdash; April, June, September, January &mdash; the IRS asks whether enough had been paid <em>by that date</em>. Not by the end of the year.</li>\n<li><b>Missing a checkpoint starts a clock.</b> If you were $1,500 short in April, a charge runs on that $1,500 from April onward. It is interest rather than a fine: the government was owed money and did not have it. Miss April, June and September and three clocks are running.</li>\n<li><b>An estimated payment carries the date you sent it.</b> December money satisfies the January checkpoint. It cannot travel back to April, so those three clocks keep running until you pay.</li>\n<li><b>Withholding is exempt from that, by law.</b> Money withheld from wages is treated as having come out evenly across all four quarters, whatever the actual dates. So $6,000 withheld in December is treated as $1,500 sitting there in April, $1,500 in June, and so on. Every checkpoint was met and no clock ever started.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is why increasing withholding can fix an underpayment from earlier in the year in a way a late estimated payment cannot.</p>\n<p>The same applies to a spouse&rsquo;s job on a joint return.</p>\n<div class="spoke"><b>Goes deeper:</b> W-2 job plus self-employment income &mdash; how much extra to set aside</div></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n<section id="after-filing">\n<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-num">09</div><h2>What To Do After You File</h2></div>\n<p class="sec-intro">How long you must keep your records, what an IRS letter usually means, and how to fix a return you&rsquo;ve already sent.</p>\n<div class="rows">\n<details class="row" id="records" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">How Long To Keep Your Records</div>\n<div class="row-s">Keep your tax records three years in most cases, six years if you left more than 25% of your income off the return. Also, keep records on any property you own for as long as you own it and three years after you sell it.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><div class="figs">\n<div><span>Standard period</span><span>3 years</span></div>\n<div><span>If income understated by more than 25%</span><span>6 years</span></div>\n<div><span>If no return was filed</span><span>No limit</span></div>\n<div><span>Property records</span><span>Until sold, plus 3 years</span></div>\n</div>\n<p>The three years run from the filing date or the due date, whichever is later.</p>\n<p>Receipts, invoices, bank and card statements, mileage logs and documentation of business purposes all count. Digital copies are acceptable, as there is no requirement to keep paper.</p>\n<p>For expenses where the business purpose isn&rsquo;t obvious from the receipt (meals, travel, mixed-use equipment), a note made at the time carries considerably more weight than a reconstruction years later.</p></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="irs-letters" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">Most IRS Letters Are Not Audits</div>\n<div class="row-s">Most letters from the IRS are computer-generated notices saying a number on your return does not match a form someone sent them, a payment is missing, or the math needs fixing. Each notice has a code in the corner saying exactly what it is about, and a date to reply by.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>A common category is a mismatch notice. This is when a form was filed reporting income to the IRS that doesn&rsquo;t appear on the return. With 1099 thresholds now higher, the reverse situation (income correctly reported with no matching form) is increasingly normal and is not itself a problem.</p>\n<p>Responding by the date on the notice generally keeps the matter administrative rather than escalating it.</p>\n<p>A notice proposing additional tax is a proposal, not a determination. It can be agreed or disputed, and disputing it is a normal process rather than an escalation.</p>\n<div class="caution">The IRS initiates contact by post. It does not open with a phone call, a text message or an email demanding immediate payment. A first contact arriving by any of those routes is a strong indicator of a scam.</div></div>\n</details>\n<details class="row" id="amending" data-t="core">\n<summary>\n<div class="row-h">How To Amend A Return You&rsquo;ve Already Filed</div>\n<div class="row-s">Form 1040-X is how you correct a return you already sent in. You generally have three years from the day you filed the original return to fix it and claim money back.</div>\n</summary>\n<div class="row-body"><p>Amendments are ordinary. Common reasons include a form arriving after filing, a deduction that was missed, or an error found later.</p>\n<p>The refund window is generally three years from the original filing date, or two years from the date the tax was paid, whichever is later. An amendment that increases tax owed does not have that window. It can be filed as soon as the error is found, and interest accrues in the meantime.</p>\n<p>Amended returns take considerably longer to process than original ones. Filing one does not by itself increase audit risk.</p></div>\n</details>\n</div></section>\n\n<footer class="about">\n  <p>This page describes how the rules work in general. It is educational and does not account for any individual&rsquo;s circumstances. Whether a particular rule applies to a particular situation is a question for a credentialed tax professional.</p>\n</footer>\n\n</div>\n';
